#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""with-suite-mutex — repo-agnostic acquiring wrapper for the machine-wide
test-suite mutex (coordinator_core.testing.suite_mutex, DR-088 layer 6).

WHY THIS EXISTS: the mutex's READ side (the bash_guards deny check) has
always worked, but its TAKE side was wired into exactly two callers in this
repo — coordinator_core/testing/full_runner.py and
coordinator/bin/validate-fast-and-packageability.py. Every other way a
suite actually gets run (a bare pytest, pnpm run tier:fast, a
consumer-repo's own runner script) took nothing, so holder() was
effectively always None and layer 6 never fired for those paths. This
wrapper closes that gap by being a generic acquire-run-release shell around
an ARBITRARY command, so any repo/runner can opt in with the same one-liner
regardless of whether it speaks pytest, pnpm, or a bespoke script.

USAGE
    with-suite-mutex [--owner-label LABEL] [--collection NAME] [--max-runtime SECS] [--reuse-fresh] -- <command> [args...]
    with-suite-mutex --wait [--wait-timeout SECS]

REUSE MODE: --reuse-fresh (a wrapper flag, before -- only) first reads
the current suite-run record of --collection. A fresh record for the
same command is reused with no spawn: stderr gets suite-run: reused ... and
the exit code is the record's. Otherwise the tier-U gate runs on the command as
typed (exit 77 on refusal), a held mutex is refused (exit 75, the --wait
advice), and the command runs once under a zero-timeout acquire (a lost race
terminates the unrun child, exit 75); a recorded run prints suite-run: ran ....

    with-suite-mutex -- pnpm run tier:fast
    with-suite-mutex -- python3 -m pytest -m 'not cadence'
    with-suite-mutex --owner-label workday-complete -- python3 bin/run-full-test-suite.py --yes

WAIT MODE: --wait blocks until the mutex has no holder, then exits 0; it
runs no command and takes nothing. It is the wait the guard's refusal names:
the bash_guards deny a suite-shaped command (wrapped or not) while another run
holds the mutex, so the wrapped form can never reach its own blocking acquire
at that moment, and a raw pid-liveness poll on the holder is denied by its own
guard. --wait is not suite-shaped, so it passes both. Exit 1 after
--wait-timeout SECS (default suite_mutex.MUTEX_WAIT_SECS) with the
holder still named. Freedom is a snapshot, not a reservation: another run can
take the mutex between this exit and the retry, which is then refused again.

Without --wait, a literal -- followed by at least one token is REQUIRED — mirrors
coordinator/bin/scoped-git-commit's own hard requirement for a -- and a
non-empty operand, and its refusal to offer an unscoped form. There is no
"everything after the flags is the command" fallback: an accidentally-eaten
flag-looking child argument must never silently swallow into this wrapper's
own flag parsing.

PID SEMANTICS (load-bearing — see suite_mutex's module docstring,
"PID-LIVENESS"): this wrapper is a short-lived acquirer that does not itself
run the suite — it spawns a child process and waits on it. The module
docstring is explicit that such a caller MUST pass pid= explicitly,
because the PID recorded in the lock's metadata is the sole liveness signal;
recording this wrapper's own os.getpid() would make the lock look dead
the instant the child inherits the terminal and this process's stack frame
returns from acquire() — in the worst case immediately if the OS reuses
the wrapper's PID for something else before the child exits. The lock is
therefore acquired with pid=child.pid, taken from the subprocess.Popen
handle after spawn.

EXIT CODE is the child's, verbatim — this wrapper never coerces a suite
failure into a wrapper success or vice versa. The ONE exception is the
--max-runtime ceiling, which kills the child and returns 124: there is no
child exit code to report in that case, because the child never produced one.

RELEASE ON EVERY EXIT PATH: normal completion, non-zero child exit, an
exception before/after spawn, and Ctrl-C/SIGINT. Achieved by running the
child inside suite_mutex.held(...)'s own context manager rather than a
hand-rolled acquire/release pair — its finally releases whenever
acquire() actually won, matching the module's own contract.

SPAWN-THEN-ACQUIRE ORDERING (the actual PID chicken/egg): acquire()
needs the child's PID at acquisition time (see PID SEMANTICS above), but the
child's PID does not exist until after it is spawned — and spawning it
unconditionally before acquiring would let two concurrent wrapper
invocations both start real work before either wins the mutex, defeating
serialization entirely. This wrapper resolves the ordering by spawning the
child, then suspending it (via psutil.Process.suspend(), the same
cross-platform primitive suite_mutex's own liveness check already
depends on for pid_alive), acquiring the mutex with the now-known child
PID, and only then resuming it.

KNOWN GAP — suspend is NOT atomic with spawn: Popen() and .suspend()
are two separate syscalls, so a child that runs to completion inside that
window (before this process reaches .suspend()) executes fully
unserialized against a concurrent invocation; suspend() then raises
NoSuchProcess, swallowed below, and the mutex is still acquired against
the correct (already-exited) child PID, so no lock-metadata corruption
results — only the serialization guarantee is weakened for such fast
children. Closing this fully would mean CREATE_SUSPENDED on Windows or a
POSIX self-SIGSTOP preexec_fn, both platform-specific spawn-flag
changes; deliberately not done here — see review escalation, and the
CREATE_NO_WINDOW negative-spec above for why touching spawn flags on this
line is treated as a tradeoff, not a silent fix.

WAIT POSTURE: copies full_runner.py's and
validate-fast-and-packageability.py's posture and message shape exactly
— wait up to suite_mutex.MUTEX_WAIT_SECS, and on timeout print the
current holder and PROCEED UNSERIALIZED with a warning, rather than
refusing. This is a resource control, not an authorization gate; refusing to
run at all here would turn a serialization aid into a spurious failure.

Negative-spec — what this wrapper is NOT:
    - NOT runner-aware. It knows nothing about pytest, pnpm, node, or any
      other test tool — it execs whatever argv follows -- unmodified.
      Do not add runner-specific flag parsing here.
    - NOT a retry loop or an output filter. It DOES capture: the child's
      stdout/stderr are pipes that two threads forward, byte for byte and
      unmodified, to this process's matching streams while appending them to
      one log, which becomes current.log of the two-slot suite-run record
      (coordinator_core.testing.suite_run_record) for --collection.
      Capture never alters, drops or reorders a forwarded byte.
    - NOT a substitute for the runner's own per-test timeout. --max-runtime
      is a ceiling on the WHOLE command and fires only when a run has stopped
      making progress entirely; a slow or hanging individual test is the
      runner's job (--timeout for pytest) and should be bounded there,
      where the failure can still name the test.
    - NOT an authorization check — it takes the same mutex the
      bash_guards deny-side already reads; it does not replace or
      duplicate tier/grant enforcement. The one exception is --reuse-fresh,
      whose miss path runs enforce_tier_u_gate itself because the Bash guard
      defers such a segment to this wrapper.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import os
import re
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import shlex
import time
from typing import List, Optional, Tuple


def _wants_help(argv: List[str]) -> bool:
    """True for `--help`/`-h` among this wrapper's own flags. Tokens after `--`
    belong to the child: `with-suite-mutex -- pytest -h` runs pytest's help."""
    flags = argv[: argv.index("--")] if "--" in argv else argv
    return "--help" in flags or "-h" in flags


# Answered before psutil and the engine bootstrap: the percolate entrypoint
# gate runs `--help` with no machine-local registry, where
# `require_engine_on_path` raises. Help text must not depend on either.
if __name__ == "__main__" and _wants_help(sys.argv[1:]):
    print(__doc__.strip())
    sys.exit(0)

import psutil  # noqa: E402

_LIB_DIR = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "lib")
if _LIB_DIR not in sys.path:
    sys.path.insert(0, _LIB_DIR)

from cc_invoke import require_colocated_engine_on_path  # noqa: E402

# The engine root must be on sys.path before the coordinator_core import
# below: this file is also published into the claude-klabauter mirror, where
# coordinator_core is NOT pip-installed and the interpreter's sys.path[0] is
# this bin/ directory, not the checkout root (and this CLI is deliberately
# repo-agnostic — resolved off this file's own location, never off cwd or
# the calling repo). Same bootstrap as coordinator/bin/coordinator-lesson-add
# (9b979ee5f).
#
# COLOCATED, never the env-first `require_engine_on_path`: what this imports is
# the engine's OWN test helper, so the right copy is the one belonging to the
# checkout this file lives in. See with-tier-t-slot's note at the same seam for
# what env-first did to a run whose COORDINATOR_ENGINE_ROOT named a mirror
# pinned behind the checkout.
require_colocated_engine_on_path(__file__)

from coordinator_core.session.tier_u_gate import enforce_tier_u_gate  # noqa: E402
from coordinator_core.testing import (  # noqa: E402
    suite_mutex,
    suite_run_record,
    suite_run_reuse,
)
from coordinator_core.win_portability import no_console_creationflags  # noqa: E402

_DEFAULT_OWNER_PREFIX = "with-suite-mutex"
_DEFAULT_COLLECTION = "default"

#: Exit codes of the --reuse-fresh refusals: 77 = tier-U gate refused, 75 = mutex held.
_GATE_REFUSED_EXIT_CODE = 77
_MUTEX_HELD_EXIT_CODE = 75

#: Wall-clock ceiling on a single wrapped command, in seconds. 0 disables it.
#:
#: Anchored to ``suite_mutex.STALE_TTL_SECS`` rather than to a number invented
#: here: that constant is already the module's own answer to "past this age a
#: holder is not live", so a child outliving it is, by the mutex's own
#: definition, no longer a holder worth waiting for. Raising one without the
#: other would let this wrapper defend a lock the lock itself has given up on.
#:
#: WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL, and why no pytest flag replaces it (measured
#: 2026-08-25 on the fast tier): `--timeout` and `--session-timeout` are both
#: enforced from inside `pytest_runtest_protocol`, i.e. only while a test is
#: RUNNING. When an xdist worker dies uninterruptibly (`node down: Not properly
#: terminated`), the controller blocks in `dsession.loop_once` -> `queue.get()`
#: and every surviving worker sits idle in `execnet ... serve()`. No test is
#: running anywhere, so neither timeout can ever fire and the run hangs forever
#: -- while holding this fleet-wide mutex, which blocks every peer's suite.
#: Only a bound OUTSIDE pytest closes that, which is what this is.
_DEFAULT_MAX_RUNTIME_SECS = suite_mutex.STALE_TTL_SECS

#: Seconds between holder() reads in ``--wait`` mode. holder() is one stat plus
#: at most one small read, and also reclaims a dead holder's lock.
_WAIT_POLL_SECS = 1.0

#: Exit code for a run killed by the ceiling. 124 is GNU ``timeout``'s, chosen
#: so an existing caller that already special-cases a timeout keeps working.
_MAX_RUNTIME_EXIT_CODE = 124


def _install_signal_handlers() -> None:
    """Route SIGTERM/SIGBREAK through the same KeyboardInterrupt cleanup path
    as Ctrl-C, so a lock acquired by this wrapper is released on every
    interrupt style a caller (or a fleet-wide process reaper) might send —
    not only a literal Ctrl-C, whose default handler already raises
    ``KeyboardInterrupt``. Best-effort: a signal absent on this platform (no
    ``SIGBREAK`` off Windows) or one the runtime refuses to rebind is a
    silent no-op, never a startup failure.
    """

    def _handler(signum, frame):  # noqa: ANN001 - stdlib signal handler shape
        raise KeyboardInterrupt()

    for name in ("SIGTERM", "SIGBREAK"):
        sig = getattr(signal, name, None)
        if sig is None:
            continue
        try:
            signal.signal(sig, _handler)
        except (ValueError, OSError):
            pass


_ParsedArgs = Tuple[Optional[str], Optional[str], float, List[str], bool]
_BAD_ARGS: _ParsedArgs = (None, None, 0.0, [], False)

_USAGE = (
    "Usage: with-suite-mutex [--owner-label LABEL] [--collection NAME] [--max-runtime SECS] "
    "[--reuse-fresh] -- <command> [args...]"
)


def _parse_args(argv: List[str]) -> _ParsedArgs:
    """Parse ``argv`` into ``(owner_label, collection, max_runtime_secs, command, reuse_fresh)``.

    ``collection`` is None unless ``--collection NAME`` was given (a slug).
    ``--reuse-fresh`` is a wrapper flag only: after ``--`` it belongs to the child.

    Returns ``(None, None, 0.0, [], False)`` (with an error already printed) on a malformed
    invocation — no ``--``, or a ``--`` with nothing after it. Every flag
    MUST appear before the ``--`` separator; nothing after it is
    interpreted, matching ``scoped-git-commit``'s own hard split.

    ``--max-runtime SECS`` overrides ``_DEFAULT_MAX_RUNTIME_SECS``; ``0``
    disables the ceiling for a caller that genuinely needs an unbounded run.
    This is NOT runner-specific flag parsing (see the negative-spec above) —
    it is a bound on the wrapped child whatever that child is.
    """
    if "--" not in argv:
        print(
            "with-suite-mutex: missing required `--` separator before the command.\n"
            + _USAGE,
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return _BAD_ARGS

    sep_index = argv.index("--")
    flags = argv[:sep_index]
    command = argv[sep_index + 1 :]

    if not command:
        print(
            "with-suite-mutex: `--` must be followed by at least one command token.\n"
            + _USAGE,
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return _BAD_ARGS

    owner_label: Optional[str] = None
    collection: Optional[str] = None
    max_runtime: float = _DEFAULT_MAX_RUNTIME_SECS
    reuse_fresh = False

    def _parse_collection(raw: str) -> Optional[str]:
        if not suite_run_record.COLLECTION_RE.match(raw):
            print(
                f"with-suite-mutex: --collection must match [a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]*, got {raw!r}.",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            return None
        return raw

    def _parse_max_runtime(raw: str) -> Optional[float]:
        try:
            value = float(raw)
        except (TypeError, ValueError):
            print(
                f"with-suite-mutex: --max-runtime needs a number of seconds, got {raw!r}.",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            return None
        if value < 0:
            print("with-suite-mutex: --max-runtime cannot be negative.", file=sys.stderr)
            return None
        return value

    i = 0
    while i < len(flags):
        flag = flags[i]
        if flag == "--reuse-fresh":
            reuse_fresh = True
            i += 1
            continue
        if flag == "--owner-label":
            if i + 1 >= len(flags):
                print("with-suite-mutex: --owner-label requires a value.", file=sys.stderr)
                return _BAD_ARGS
            owner_label = flags[i + 1]
            i += 2
            continue
        if flag.startswith("--owner-label="):
            owner_label = flag.split("=", 1)[1]
            i += 1
            continue
        if flag == "--collection" or flag.startswith("--collection="):
            if flag == "--collection":
                if i + 1 >= len(flags):
                    print("with-suite-mutex: --collection requires a value.", file=sys.stderr)
                    return _BAD_ARGS
                raw, i = flags[i + 1], i + 2
            else:
                raw, i = flag.split("=", 1)[1], i + 1
            collection = _parse_collection(raw)
            if collection is None:
                return _BAD_ARGS
            continue
        if flag == "--max-runtime":
            if i + 1 >= len(flags):
                print("with-suite-mutex: --max-runtime requires a value.", file=sys.stderr)
                return _BAD_ARGS
            parsed = _parse_max_runtime(flags[i + 1])
            if parsed is None:
                return _BAD_ARGS
            max_runtime = parsed
            i += 2
            continue
        if flag.startswith("--max-runtime="):
            parsed = _parse_max_runtime(flag.split("=", 1)[1])
            if parsed is None:
                return _BAD_ARGS
            max_runtime = parsed
            i += 1
            continue
        print(f"with-suite-mutex: unrecognized flag {flag!r}.", file=sys.stderr)
        return _BAD_ARGS

    return owner_label, collection, max_runtime, command, reuse_fresh


def _parse_wait_args(argv: List[str]) -> Optional[float]:
    """Return the ``--wait`` timeout in seconds, or ``None`` when ``argv`` is
    not a wait-mode invocation (no ``--wait`` flag among the wrapper's own
    flags). Raises ``ValueError`` with a printable message on a malformed one.

    Wait mode runs no command, so it rejects a ``--`` and any flag other than
    ``--wait-timeout``: ``--owner-label``/``--max-runtime`` describe a wrapped
    child that does not exist here.
    """
    if "--wait" not in argv:
        return None
    timeout = suite_mutex.MUTEX_WAIT_SECS
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        flag = argv[i]
        if flag == "--wait":
            i += 1
            continue
        if flag == "--wait-timeout" or flag.startswith("--wait-timeout="):
            if flag == "--wait-timeout":
                if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                    raise ValueError("--wait-timeout requires a value.")
                raw, i = argv[i + 1], i + 2
            else:
                raw, i = flag.split("=", 1)[1], i + 1
            try:
                timeout = float(raw)
            except ValueError:
                raise ValueError(
                    f"--wait-timeout needs a number of seconds, got {raw!r}."
                ) from None
            if timeout < 0:
                raise ValueError("--wait-timeout cannot be negative.")
            continue
        raise ValueError(
            f"--wait takes no command and only --wait-timeout SECS, got {flag!r}."
        )
    return timeout


def _slug(text: str) -> str:
    """``text`` lowered and reduced to a ``COLLECTION_RE`` slug; empty when nothing survives."""
    return re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9._-]+", "-", text.lower()).strip("-._")


def _default_collection(owner_label: Optional[str], command: List[str]) -> str:
    """Suite name when ``--collection`` is absent: the owner label, else the command's
    leading tokens up to the first flag (at most three), as a slug.
    Commands that differ only after a flag (``python -m pytest a`` vs ``... b``) share a name;
    pass ``--collection`` or ``--owner-label`` to separate them.
    Falls back to ``_DEFAULT_COLLECTION`` when no slug survives."""
    name = _slug(owner_label or "")
    if not name:
        words = []
        for token in command[:3]:
            if token.startswith("-"):
                break
            base = os.path.basename(token)
            words.append(base[:-4] if base.lower().endswith(".exe") else base)
        name = _slug("-".join(words))[:40].strip("-._")
    return name if name and suite_run_record.COLLECTION_RE.match(name) else _DEFAULT_COLLECTION


def _wait_for_free(timeout: float) -> int:
    """Block until ``suite_mutex.holder()`` is None; 0 when free, 1 on timeout."""
    deadline = time.monotonic() + timeout
    while True:
        current = suite_mutex.holder()
        if current is None:
            return 0
        if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            print(
                "with-suite-mutex: still held after %gs by pid %s | owner %s | "
                "started %s | %s"
                % (
                    timeout, current.get("pid", "?"), current.get("owner", "?"),
                    current.get("started_at", "?"), current.get("cmd", "?"),
                ),
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            return 1
        time.sleep(min(_WAIT_POLL_SECS, max(0.0, deadline - time.monotonic())))


def _kill_process_tree(process: "subprocess.Popen[bytes]") -> int:
    """Terminate ``process`` AND its descendants; return how many were killed.

    Killing only the direct child is not enough for any runner that spawns
    its own workers. pytest-xdist is the case this was written against: the
    controller is the direct child, but the worker processes are ITS
    children, and they do not exit when it dies -- they sit in
    ``execnet ... serve()`` waiting for a controller that is gone, holding
    real memory on a box with dozens of concurrent sessions. Observed
    2026-08-25: killing the controller by hand left six orphaned workers
    that each had to be killed individually.

    Best-effort by construction: a process that exits between enumeration
    and signalling raises ``NoSuchProcess``, which is the outcome we wanted
    anyway, and one we cannot signal raises ``AccessDenied``. Neither is
    worth failing the kill path over, so both are swallowed per-process.
    """
    try:
        parent = psutil.Process(process.pid)
        victims = parent.children(recursive=True) + [parent]
    except psutil.Error:
        victims = []

    for victim in victims:
        try:
            victim.terminate()
        except (psutil.NoSuchProcess, psutil.AccessDenied):
            pass
    _gone, alive = psutil.wait_procs(victims, timeout=10)
    for victim in alive:
        try:
            victim.kill()
        except (psutil.NoSuchProcess, psutil.AccessDenied):
            pass
    psutil.wait_procs(alive, timeout=5)

    if process.poll() is None:
        try:
            process.wait(timeout=5)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            pass
    return len(victims)


def _wait_interruptible(
    process: "subprocess.Popen[bytes]", max_runtime: float = 0.0
) -> int:
    """Wait for ``process`` to exit while staying responsive to signals.

    ``Popen.wait()`` with no timeout blocks inside a single uninterruptible
    OS wait call on Windows (``WaitForSingleObject`` with an infinite
    timeout) — a signal delivered mid-wait (Ctrl-C, Ctrl-Break, SIGTERM) is
    not observed by the Python interpreter until that call returns, which
    defeats this wrapper's release-on-interrupt contract. Polling with a
    short timeout instead gives the interpreter a periodic chance to notice
    a pending signal and raise, on every platform.

    ``max_runtime`` (seconds; ``0`` disables) is a wall-clock ceiling on the
    child. On expiry the child's whole process tree is killed and
    ``_MAX_RUNTIME_EXIT_CODE`` is returned, so the surrounding
    ``suite_mutex.held(...)`` unwinds normally and RELEASES the lock. That
    release is the point: the failure this bounds is a child that stops
    making progress while still holding a machine-wide mutex, which converts
    one session's wedge into every peer's.
    """
    deadline = (time.monotonic() + max_runtime) if max_runtime > 0 else None
    while True:
        try:
            return process.wait(timeout=0.25)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            if deadline is not None and time.monotonic() >= deadline:
                killed = _kill_process_tree(process)
                print(
                    "with-suite-mutex: killed the command after "
                    f"{int(max_runtime)}s ({killed} process(es)) and released the "
                    "suite mutex. The ceiling cannot tell a wedged run from a long "
                    "serial one; output above is unbuffered. Bound workers via "
                    "coordinator-resolve-validation-cmd --full, or if wedged: "
                    "py-spy dump --pid <controller-pid>. "
                    "Raise or disable this ceiling with --max-runtime SECS (0 = unbounded).",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                )
                return _MAX_RUNTIME_EXIT_CODE
            continue


def main(argv: List[str]) -> int:
    _install_signal_handlers()
    try:
        wait_timeout = _parse_wait_args(argv[: argv.index("--")] if "--" in argv else argv)
    except ValueError as exc:
        print(f"with-suite-mutex: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if wait_timeout is not None:
        if "--" in argv:
            print("with-suite-mutex: --wait takes no `-- <command>`.", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        return _wait_for_free(wait_timeout)
    owner_label, collection, max_runtime, command, reuse_fresh = _parse_args(argv)
    if not command:
        return 2
    argv_as_given = list(command)
    if collection is None:
        collection = _default_collection(owner_label, argv_as_given)

    prefix = owner_label if owner_label else _DEFAULT_OWNER_PREFIX
    owner = suite_mutex.mutex_owner(prefix)
    cmd_str = " ".join(command)

    # The child's stdout/stderr are pipes this wrapper forwards live (see _Tee), so
    # CREATE_NO_WINDOW is safe: its hidden console is harmless once both streams
    # are explicit pipes. Passing the flag with real fds instead would collide
    # with the pipe kwargs.
    # CreateProcess does no PATHEXT resolution, so a bare `pnpm`/`npm`/`yarn`/
    # `npx` -- all of which ship on Windows as `.cmd` shims -- raises
    # WinError 2 here while resolving fine from a shell. shutil.which honours
    # PATHEXT and returns the shim's real path. Resolution is deliberately
    # fail-open: which() returning None leaves `command` untouched so a
    # genuinely-absent binary still surfaces Popen's own FileNotFoundError
    # naming it, rather than a substituted error from this lookup. shell=True
    # is NOT the fix -- it would re-parse the already-split argv and mangle any
    # argument containing a space or quote.
    resolved = shutil.which(command[0])
    if resolved is not None:
        command = [resolved, *command[1:]]

    repo_root = suite_run_record.repo_root_for(os.getcwd())
    if repo_root is None:
        print("with-suite-mutex: not inside a git repo; no suite-run record.", file=sys.stderr)
    if reuse_fresh:
        reuse = (
            suite_run_reuse.find_reusable(repo_root, collection, argv_as_given)
            if repo_root is not None else None
        )
        if reuse is not None:
            print(suite_run_reuse.reused_line(reuse), file=sys.stderr)
            return reuse.exit_code
        gate = enforce_tier_u_gate(
            shlex.join(argv_as_given),
            repo_root=str(repo_root) if repo_root is not None else None,
        )
        if not gate.proceed:
            print(gate.refusal_message, file=sys.stderr)
            return _GATE_REFUSED_EXIT_CODE
        if suite_mutex.holder() is not None:
            print(
                "with-suite-mutex: another suite run holds the mutex; run "
                "`with-suite-mutex --wait`, then retry.",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            return _MUTEX_HELD_EXIT_CODE
    process = subprocess.Popen(
        command,
        stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
        stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
        **no_console_creationflags(),
    )
    try:
        ps_process = psutil.Process(process.pid)
    except psutil.Error:
        # Review: coordinator:code-reviewer -- Popen() succeeded but the
        # child was already gone/transitioning by the time we looked it up
        # (extremely fast exit). Reap it like every other exit path does,
        # rather than propagating with no cleanup at all.
        if process.poll() is None:
            process.terminate()
            try:
                process.wait(timeout=10)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                process.kill()
                process.wait()
        raise

    def _resume_then_escalate() -> None:
        """Resume-then-escalate cleanup for a child that may still be
        SUSPENDED when we're forced off the happy path.

        Review: coordinator:code-reviewer -- a process stopped via
        ``psutil.Process.suspend()`` (SIGSTOP on POSIX) does not act on a
        pending SIGTERM until it is resumed or killed outright; resuming
        first is required or ``terminate()`` below is silently held pending
        forever, leaving a suspended orphan for the lock's 45-minute stale
        TTL.
        """
        try:
            ps_process.resume()
        except (psutil.NoSuchProcess, psutil.AccessDenied):
            pass
        if process.poll() is None:
            process.terminate()
            try:
                process.wait(timeout=10)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                process.kill()
                process.wait()

    try:
        ps_process.suspend()
    except psutil.NoSuchProcess:
        # Exited (or never truly scheduled) before we could suspend it --
        # nothing left to serialize against; fall through and let
        # process.wait() report its real exit code below.
        pass
    except psutil.AccessDenied:
        # Cannot suspend on this platform/process -- degrade to "spawned
        # already running" rather than failing the whole invocation; the
        # mutex is still acquired against the correct (child) PID.
        pass

    try:
        with suite_mutex.held(
            owner, cmd_str,
            timeout=0.0 if reuse_fresh else suite_mutex.MUTEX_WAIT_SECS,
            pid=process.pid,
        ) as acquired:
            if not acquired and reuse_fresh:
                # SIGKILL reaches a stopped child; SIGTERM would stay pending on it.
                process.kill()
                process.wait()
                print(
                    "with-suite-mutex: another suite run took the mutex first; run "
                    "`with-suite-mutex --wait`, then retry.",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                )
                return _MUTEX_HELD_EXIT_CODE
            if not acquired:
                current = suite_mutex.holder() or {}
                print(
                    "[WARN] suite mutex held by %s for %ss — proceeding unserialized"
                    % (current.get("owner", "<unknown>"), int(suite_mutex.MUTEX_WAIT_SECS)),
                    file=sys.stderr,
                )
            started_at = suite_run_reuse._now_iso()
            started_mono = time.monotonic()
            head_sha, dirty_paths = (None, [])
            log_path: Optional[str] = None
            if repo_root is not None:
                head_sha, dirty_paths, log_path = suite_run_reuse.begin_run(
                    repo_root, collection
                )
            try:
                ps_process.resume()
            except (psutil.NoSuchProcess, psutil.AccessDenied):
                pass
            tee = suite_run_reuse.Tee(process, log_path)
            exit_code, outcome = 130, "interrupted"
            try:
                exit_code = _wait_interruptible(process, max_runtime)
                outcome = (
                    "pass" if exit_code == 0
                    else "timeout" if exit_code == _MAX_RUNTIME_EXIT_CODE
                    else "fail"
                )
                return exit_code
            except BaseException:
                _resume_then_escalate()
                raise
            finally:
                complete = tee.finish()
                if repo_root is not None and log_path is not None:
                    suite_run_reuse.record_run(
                        str(repo_root), collection, command, head_sha, dirty_paths,
                        started_at, started_mono, exit_code, outcome, bool(acquired),
                        log_path, not complete,
                    )
                    if reuse_fresh:
                        print(
                            suite_run_reuse.ran_line(
                                collection, head_sha, outcome,
                                suite_run_record.record_dir(repo_root, collection)
                                / "current.log",
                            ),
                            file=sys.stderr,
                        )
    except BaseException:
        # Review: coordinator:code-reviewer -- covers KeyboardInterrupt AND
        # every other exception (lock-file I/O error, corrupt meta.json,
        # a non-NoSuchProcess/AccessDenied raise out of resume() above,
        # etc). The previous bare `finally` only `terminate()`d without
        # resuming first, which left a SIGSTOP'd child permanently unable
        # to observe that SIGTERM -- an unreaped, suspended orphan.
        _resume_then_escalate()
        raise


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
